---
// Why TLB page, built from content-plans/why-tlb.md — section choices and
// every "don't invent this" call trace back to that document's 🚩 list.
//
// ── UPDATED to Brand Foundation v2.0 (September 2026) ────────────────────
// This page was the worst-affected on the site. It opened with an H1 that
// v2.0 rules out twice over, on the page whose own argument is that TLB is
// more honest than the alternatives. §1, §2, §5, §6, §7 and §8 were all
// rewritten; each carries its own ✅ note saying what changed and which rule
// required it.
//
// BLOCKERS, as they now stand:
//  1. ✅ RESOLVED — "Nine local mums" in the H1 and meta description. v2.0
//     §2.4 settles it: the full-time headcount "is not accurate and it has
//     been removed from all page copy", and §9.3 keeps "local mums" out of
//     positioning copy entirely. The H1 is now §1.1 verbatim. This page and
//     the homepage no longer disagree, because neither makes the claim.
//  2. ✅ RESOLVED — branded FAQ "Am I locked into anything?" was [TBC] and
//     held back. v2.0 §11.5 supplies the answer in full, so it is published
//     below. The plan's point was right: on a page teaching contract
//     literacy, silence read as an answer.
//  3. ⚠️ STILL OPEN — the four non-branded FAQs assert checkable numbers
//     about third-party contracts and cite an unnamed "published case". They
//     are the page's strongest asset, but §12.2 forbids unverifiable
//     statistics. They need a substantiation pass before launch.
//  4. ✅ RESOLVED — §6's comparison table. See its own comment block.
//
// ⚠️ STILL OPEN, and now the only thing on this page that blocks launch
// besides 3: the hero has no photograph. This page argues "the part none of
// the others can show you: names and faces", and promises them three times.
// A hero with no faces on it is the weakest possible opening for that
// argument. §12.1 is blunt about which asset does the work here.
//
// Two component extensions were added for this page: `kicker` on Hero.astro
// and on CallToAction.astro (§1 and §14 both need an eyebrow label). Both
// are additive and default to rendering nothing.
//
// ── SEO COPY REWRITE, 17 September 2026 (workbook row 53) ────────────────
// Against the Why TLB copy-rewrite brief dated 16 September 2026. Mapped
// primary `best house cleaning services near me` 70/mo; secondaries
// `best window cleaning service near me` 30/mo, `best carpet cleaning
// services` 20/mo, `best bond cleaning services` 10/mo, `female cleaners`
// 0/mo (Keyword Mapping & Metadata workbook, 15 Sep 2026). No live pull and
// no connected Search Console property, so no position or click figure is
// recorded anywhere.
//
// This is by far the LIGHTEST rewrite in the engagement and that is the
// finding. Twenty of the brief's twenty-five sections are marked no change,
// including every body section without exception. The URL already matched
// the workbook, the title already matched it character for character, and
// the live meta description was already more compliant than the approved
// one. Four things changed:
//   §1  H1 now carries the mapped primary; "Local team, run properly." is
//       demoted one line into `subheading` rather than cut.
//   §1  hero button is no longer `Get an instant quote` — this page sells no
//       service — and now carries the approved CTA's imperative core.
//   §12 both FAQ headings (one generic, one Title Case).
//   §14 closing lead now opens with the approved CTA sentence.
//
// NOT DONE, deliberately. The brief maps three service keywords (window,
// carpet, bond) to this page and none of those services is mentioned on it.
// Nothing here chases them: writing window, carpet and bond copy onto the
// site's best page to capture 60/mo that belongs on the window, carpet and
// end-of-lease pages would wreck it. Re-primarying row 53 is a workbook
// change request, not a page edit.
//
// The brief's first blocker — "is `noindex, nofollow` deliberate?" — is
// ANSWERED and it is not a page setting. src/data/site-env.ts puts the tag
// on every page unless SITE_ENV=production, so the brief was reading a
// staging build. Nothing on this page needs changing for it.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/sections/SiteHeader.astro';
import Hero from '../components/sections/Hero.astro';
import TrustBar from '../components/sections/TrustBar.astro';
import Backdrop from '../components/ui/Backdrop.astro';
import TextBlock from '../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import PathwayCards from '../components/sections/PathwayCards.astro';
import ContentGrid from '../components/sections/ContentGrid.astro';
import Callout from '../components/sections/Callout.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '../components/sections/ComparisonTable.astro';
import ServiceBlocks from '../components/sections/ServiceBlocks.astro';
import GoogleReviewSlider from '../components/sections/GoogleReviewSlider.astro';
import { googleBusinessProfileUrl, googleReviews } from '../data/googleReviews';
import Faq from '../components/sections/Faq.astro';
import CallToAction from '../components/sections/CallToAction.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/sections/SiteFooter.astro';
import logoImg from '../assets/logo/tlb-logo-horizontal.png';
// §2 backdrop, behind the Cream scrim (this page's own folder).
// §5's resolution line — the TLB team at work, since "we built TLB" is a
// statement about them. Not this page's own why_tlb photo again: that one is
// already behind §2 above, and one photo twice on one page reads as a mistake.
import footerLogoImg from '../assets/logo/tlb-logo-square.png';
import {
  primaryNav,
  headerNav,
  headerSecondaryCta,
  footerServiceLinks,
  footerContact,
  footerCopyright,
  quoteCta,
} from '../data/navigation';
import { comparisonColumns, comparisonRows } from '../data/comparison';
import { trustBarCards } from '../data/trust';
import { localBusiness } from '../data/business';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
import { getPage } from '../lib/cms';
import * as section from '../lib/cms-schemas';
import { sized } from '../lib/cms-schemas';

// In `pageMeta`, on the description:
// ✅ RESOLVED (Brand Foundation v2.0) — was "Nine local mums, no anonymous
// contractors and no franchise fees", which breached three rules in one
// sentence: §2.4 (the full-time headcount is not accurate), §9.3 ("local
// mums" is out of positioning copy) and §9.4 (competitor framing belongs in
// the table, not in prose, and a meta description is prose).
//
// The 16 Sep 2026 copy brief reaches the same wording independently and
// asks the workbook to adopt it: the approved row-53 meta still opens with
// the banned phrase, still carries the headcount, and is rejected by
// scripts/brand-lint.mjs. This line is 131 characters, keeps the approved
// CTA verbatim, and is the one to put back into the workbook. TITLE and
// URL both already match row 53 exactly, so neither is touched.

// §1 Hero. The brief's overview runs 48 words against a 15–25 target and is
// three sentences; per the plan it's SPLIT, not cut — sentence 1 is the lead
// here, sentences 2 and 3 open §2's TextBlock. Nothing is lost.
//
// `kicker` is the new Hero prop: "WHY TLB" sits above the <h1>, outside it.
// Folding it into headingLines would make the heading read "WHY TLB Local
// team, run properly."
//
// Hero style matches the homepage, house cleaning and deep cleaning pages:
// `split-single-image`, copy left, image right. NO ASSET YET — `image` is
// passed with a `label` but no `src`, so Placeholder renders its dashed
// briefing box in the panel. That is a deliberate reversal of this page's
// earlier call (`minimal`, to avoid a dashed box standing in for a photo):
// the style is now shared across the site, so the slot is held open here
// instead of this page opening differently from every other one.
//
// ⚠️ The dashed box is a pre-launch state. It must not ship to production —
// either the asset lands or this hero goes back to `variant="minimal"`.
//
// A plain photograph arrived (the made bed), so `imageFit` is left at its
// `cover` default and the photo bleeds edge to edge, same as the homepage.
// If a cut-out figure on a green circle replaces it, set `imageFit` back to
// contain-bottom so the backdrop is never cropped.
//
// ⚠️ This page argues "the part none of the others can show you: names and
// faces". A hero with no faces on it is the weakest possible opening for
// that argument, so a real team photo does more work here than almost
// anywhere else.
// ✅ RESOLVED (Brand Foundation v2.0) — the H1 was "Nine local mums, and no
// anonymous contractors." and the lead was "We grew to nine full-time staff
// and won contracts with the region's leading real estate agencies without
// ever running an advertisement."
//
// Four separate breaches, on the page whose own argument is that TLB is more
// honest than the alternatives:
//   - "Nine" and "full-time" (§2.4: not accurate, and removed everywhere)
//   - "local mums" in an <h1> (§9.3: not in positioning copy)
//   - "no anonymous contractors" (§9.4: competitor framing out of prose)
//   - the agency-contracts claim (§2.4 lists it as unconfirmed, and the
//     wording has to match whether these are formal preferred-supplier
//     arrangements or ongoing contracts)
//
// The replacement H1 is §1.1 verbatim. The lead keeps the ONE claim §2.4
// marks confirmed ("Grown by word of mouth, without advertising") and drops
// the two it does not.
//
// ⚙️ SEO REWRITE (17 Sep 2026) — the H1 now carries the mapped primary
// `best house cleaning services near me` (70/mo) as a contiguous phrase, and
// "Local team, run properly." moves one line down into `subheading`. It is
// demoted, not cut: it is §3.1 in four words and §5 below is built directly
// on it.
//
// And this one was forced rather than landed, which is worth recording. "Near
// me" is a searcher's phrasing, not a writer's, and it only scans in a
// headline that is echoing the query — which is why "and how to choose one"
// is load-bearing rather than decorative. It is also honest about what the
// page does, which is compare four business models rather than assert an
// answer. The cost is that it now reads like a listicle headline on a page
// that is not a listicle, and that mismatch is the clearest evidence for the
// re-primary request on row 53. IF ROW 53 IS RE-PRIMARIED, revert this: the
// old headline goes straight back up and the subheading slot goes away.
//
// The LEAD IS DELIBERATELY UNCHANGED. Rule 1 also wants the primary in the
// opening paragraph and it is scored 0 here rather than fudged. Landing it
// would mean padding the one paragraph on the site that states the single
// fact §2.4 marks confirmed — grown by word of mouth, without advertising —
// with a query string. Two sentences, both earning their place.
//
// The BUTTON is no longer `quoteCta`. `Get an instant quote` is the regular
// cleaning CTA and this page sells no service at all; it is a navigation and
// persuasion page whose job is to send people to the service pages. Per the
// brief's Rule 4 the approved CTA sentence (72 characters) lands as the
// closing block's lead-in at §14, and the button takes its imperative core.
//
// ⚠️ Raised and not resolved: "See how TLB compares" is a promise about what
// the reader will find on THIS page, so as an on-page button it can only
// scroll them to a section they can already see. That is a category problem
// with the workbook's CTA column, which assumes a service page with an action
// at the end of it. The anchor target is the honest reading of it.

// §2 Thesis + definition. Entries 1–2 are the hero overflow (see §1).
// Entry 3 is the answer-target block — it names all four competitor
// categories in one sentence, which is what the rest of the page unpacks.
// ✅ RESOLVED (Brand Foundation v2.0) — entry 3 was "It employs nine full-time
// cleaners rather than subcontracting, which is the main difference between
// TLB and the national franchises, booking platforms and sole operators
// working the same area."
//
// Two breaches: the headcount and "full-time" (§2.4), and naming three
// competitor categories in a definition sentence (§9.4, "no characterising
// what a national brand or an independent cleaner fails to do, in running
// text"). §10.2 is the sharper one here: the sentence lumped sole operators
// into the thing TLB is better than, and "the reader has probably had a good
// one".
//
// The replacement is still an answer-target block (§14) — it states what TLB
// is, where it works and how it runs, in a form an AI assistant can lift
// whole. It just no longer defines TLB by what other people are.
//
// Geography per §2.1: the Northern Rivers leads and is the identity, the
// Tweed and the Southern Gold Coast are extensions. The old line said
// "Northern Rivers and Southern Gold Coast", which skipped the Tweed
// entirely — and §2.1 is explicit that Kingscliff, Pottsville and Tweed Heads
// are "neither Northern Rivers nor Gold Coast to anyone who lives there".

// §3 Routing cards. Unlike the other pages' cards, all three route WITHIN
// this page (or to the team page) rather than away from it — this page is
// long, and these are its table of contents.
//
// Section heading/accent and the three CTA labels are NOT in the brief.
//
// In `pathwayCards`, on card 3 ("Who would actually come?"):
// ⚠️ This promises named faces. So does §7's "neither can introduce
// you to her", and so does §14's closing CTA. Three promises pointing
// at /about/ — confirm that page will actually show the team named and
// photographed, or the page's central claim doesn't land. (Headcount
// deliberately not stated here: v2.0 §2.4.)

// §5 The trade-off — SETUP → two-sided CONTRAST → RESOLUTION, built as three
// sections rather than one prose block. The two middle paragraphs are
// deliberately parallel ("solved X / what you give up is Y"), so putting them
// side by side lets the reader see the symmetry before reading a word.
// ✅ REWRITTEN (Brand Foundation v2.0 §9.4) — this section used to be built
// on an explicit two-sided competitor contrast: "Until recently you had to
// pick one: convenience or a person you know", then one cell on what a
// platform makes you give up and one on what an independent cleaner makes you
// give up.
//
// §9.4 rules out that whole construction, and names this exact phrasing:
// "No 'nobody turns up'. No 'you have to choose'. No characterising what a
// national brand or an independent cleaner fails to do, in running text."
// §10.2 adds the sharper objection to the second cell: "Never suggest an
// independent cleaner does worse work, because the reader has probably had a
// good one... In this region that is a real person the reader knows."
//
// The structure survives intact — setup, two parallel cells, dark resolution.
// Only the subject changes. It now describes TLB's two halves (§3.1) instead
// of two competitors' two weaknesses, so the section says what TLB does and
// the comparison table immediately below it at §6 carries the comparison.
// That is §9.4's prescribed swap: "Same point, reads as information."
//
// Copy is §3.1 and §3.3 almost verbatim, including the closing line, which is
// the brand document's own sanctioned way to make this point.
//
// The setup used to be a standalone left-aligned TextBlock band above the
// grid. It is now ContentGrid's own `heading` and `lead`, so the section
// reads as one headed card grid, matching about.astro's values block.
//
// Cards 3 and 4 were added by client request (Sept 2026) and are two of
// about.astro's four values, copied word for word — including the client's
// rewrite of "Family first" into a local credential written from the
// customer's side. Edit them on both pages or they will drift. The other two
// about-page values are deliberately NOT here: "Local, and locally owned"
// restates card 1, and "The last five minutes" is about the clean itself
// rather than the two halves this section is built on.
//
// "Family first" is only safe on this page in its rewritten form. Brand
// Foundation v2.0 §2.2, quoted at §8 below, moved the school-pick-up and
// flexible-hours framing OFF this page because this page is positioning. The
// card as written makes no claim about rosters, flexibility or the workforce,
// so it does not reopen that. Do not restore the older wording here.
//
// ⚠️ Card 4 states TLB's cleaners are employees, not contractors. Same claim
// as definitionBody, the comparison table and about.astro, and it carries
// legal weight in Australia. Confirm it holds for every current employee. It
// also restates definitionBody's second line and a comparison table row, so
// a reader meets the point three times on one scroll.

// Dark, because it's the pivot the whole page turns on — and this page runs
// long on centred light prose, so it needs the tonal break.
//
// This line is §3.1's own closing sentence, used verbatim. §9.4 bans "you
// have to choose" as a claim ABOUT competitors; §3.1 makes it about the
// reader's assumption and is the brand document's approved wording.

// §6 Comparison table — the SAME table as the homepage's §8, sharing its
// columns and rows from src/data/comparison.ts. That copy was supplied
// directly by TLB with desktop and mobile mockups, so it is approved content.
//
// Only the heading and cornerLabel are set here; the substance is shared, so
// editing a row updates both pages at once.
//
// ✅ RESOLVED — "THE PAGE NOW PROMISES MORE THAN THE TABLE DELIVERS." The
// shared table used to be two columns (TLB vs "Other Brands") with TLB
// ticking every row and Other Brands crossing every row, which distinguished
// none of the models §3's card 1 promises and showed nothing being right for
// somebody else. src/data/comparison.ts now holds the homepage's approved
// three-column prose table instead, so a national brand or app and an
// independent cleaner get a column each, TLB does not win every row, and the
// cells state what happens rather than scoring it. Card 1's copy needs no
// softening: the table now delivers what it promises.
//
// ✅ RESOLVED — the "Owned and run by local mums from the Northern Rivers"
// row is gone with the rest of the old table (v2.0 §9.3).
//
// This table is now carrying MORE weight than it used to. §5 and §7 above
// were rewritten to stop characterising competitors in prose (§9.4), and this
// is where that argument went. Do not thin these rows out.
//
// In `comparisonTable`, on "How TLB Cleaning compares":
// Deliberately NOT the homepage's "Why TLB Cleaning is different" — that
// would restate the page's own name inside it. Reframed for this context.

// §7 The model explanation. Straight prose, no list, no image. Don't break
// it up.
//
// ✅ REWRITTEN (Brand Foundation v2.0 §9.4) — this was two paragraphs
// explaining how a franchise network and a booking platform work, closing on
// "Neither can introduce you to her." Genuinely good writing, and it was the
// strongest thing in the v1.0 brief. It is also a paragraph of running text
// whose entire subject is what two competitor models cannot do, which is the
// one thing §9.4 rules out: "A page that spends its time on what other people
// get wrong sounds defensive, and invites the reader to wonder who we are
// arguing with."
//
// The point is not lost. It moved. "Who cleans your home" and "When someone
// is away" are now rows one and two of the comparison table at §6, where
// §9.4 says a comparison belongs and where it "reads as information rather
// than complaint".
//
// What replaces it is the same argument made positively, and it keeps the
// heading, which still works: a brand can be bought, a person cannot.

// §8 Why people stay.
//
// ✅ RESOLVED (Brand Foundation v2.0 §2.2) — "Our team is built around school
// pick-up, because most of us are doing it" was the mother-led framing
// arriving two thirds of the way down a positioning page, and it used to be
// the thing that made this page's H1 ("Nine local mums") disagree with its
// own body copy ("most of us").
//
// §2.2 does not delete that story, it relocates it: it belongs on the about
// page, the team section, social content and recruitment copy, and NOT in
// "service page positioning". This page is positioning. The flexible-hours
// mechanism is genuinely true and genuinely persuasive, so it should be told
// properly at /about/ and /work-with-us/ rather than deleted here.
//
// What stays is the part a client is actually buying (§1.5): people stay, so
// you are not meeting somebody new every fortnight. The retention argument is
// §3.1's, and it survives without describing the workforce.
//
// ⚠️ STILL OPEN — "Cleaning has a staff turnover problem across the whole
// industry" is an industry claim with no source supplied. Same substantiation
// question as the non-branded FAQs below. §12.1 wants numbers, but only once
// confirmed.

// §9a Switching guide intro.

// §9b Four objection cards. The objections keep their quote marks — the
// voice is the reader's own, and losing the quotes loses that.
//
// ⚠️ Card 3 contains the page's ONLY soft sell ("Book us once as a one-off"),
// placed inside advice that warns against being sold to. That's deliberate
// and it works. Don't let a later edit add a second pitch to the other three.
//
// In `objectionBlocks`, on the card headed "She lives around here. I will run into her.":
// "Tegan" in the source → "Teagan", the settled spelling.

// §9c The timing callout.
//
// Now a real Callout (tone="warning"). It shipped as a dark TextBlock while
// Callout.astro had only one consumer and this library's bar is two; the
// NDIS page's "you probably don't need a registered provider" box became the
// second, so the component was built and this was retro-fitted to it.

// §10 Agencies and businesses.
//
// ⚠️ hrefs are a PROPOSAL — the brief supplies none. The commercial slug in
// particular should be checked against the header mega-menu before launch.
// Icon-less, same as every other page: ServiceIcon.astro's set is abstract
// and has no agency/office glyph.

// §12 Branded FAQs — now 4 of 4.
//
// ✅ RESOLVED — "Am I locked into anything?" was held back on a [TBC] reading
// "confirm commercial terms before publishing. This is the single most
// important question on this page and it cannot be answered with a guess."
// It is answered below, and the answer is not a guess: Brand Foundation v2.0
// §3.2 and §11.5 both state the commercial terms as settled fact ("No
// minimum term, no exit fee. Skip a visit when you are away and your spot
// stays yours"). §11 exists precisely so these answers stop being invented
// page by page.
//
// This mattered more than a normal hold-back. On a page that spends its
// length teaching people to read their cleaning contract, omitting the
// question about TLB's own contract was itself an answer.
//
// ⚠️ STILL WORTH CHECKING before launch: non-branded FAQ 3 below warns
// readers about non-solicitation clauses. Confirm TLB's own terms don't
// contain one, or the page argues against itself.
//
// All four below answer by CONCEDING something. That's the page's voice
// working — keep any additions in the same register.
//
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "Do you cover everywhere a national brand does?":
// ⚠️ "Five Northern Rivers council areas" — a specific number that
// appears nowhere else on the site, and the three suburb lists already in
// the codebase disagree with each other. Naming the five would also feed
// the `near me` focus keyword, which has almost nothing else on this page
// to attach to.

// §13 Non-branded FAQs — all four drafted, and the page's strongest SEO asset:
// nobody else in this market answers "what is in my cleaning contract".
//
// ⚠️ ALSO ITS BIGGEST RISK. Every answer asserts checkable numbers about
// third-party contracts — "14 or 30 days", "24 to 48 hours", "72 hours or
// five working days", "25 per cent up to the full amount" — and FAQ 3 cites
// "one published case" without naming or linking the source. None of it is
// bracketed [TBC], so it reads ready to ship. It needs a substantiation pass
// first: sources recorded for the ranges, and a decision on whether to cite
// the non-solicitation example or soften it to "some businesses".
//
// The Australian Consumer Law characterisation in FAQ 4 is sound as a general
// statement of the consumer guarantees.

// §14 Closing CTA.
//
// ⚠️ Deliberately NOT "get a quote", unlike this page's three siblings — it
// hands off to the team page instead. That is the right call for a page whose
// argument is "you can see who we are", and it should survive review even
// though it breaks the site pattern.
//
// cta.label is not in the brief; "Meet the team" chosen by TLB. Note it
// repeats the kicker directly above it, and card 3's CTA further up the page
// uses the same words — deliberate consistency, not an oversight.
//
// ⚠️ Third promise on this page of named faces at /about/. Confirm that page
// delivers them.
//
// ⚙️ SEO REWRITE (17 Sep 2026) — the approved CTA is a 72-character sentence
// rather than a button label, so per the brief's Rule 4 it lands here as the
// lead-in and the hero button above carries its imperative core. Both come
// from the same approved text, so the approved CTA is on the page.
//
// ⚠️ And it reads awkwardly, which is the flag rather than an oversight: a
// reader who has reached the bottom of this page has already seen how TLB
// compares. Placed as approved, and raised.
//
// The BUTTON stays "Meet the team" and this is a considered exception to the
// one-CTA rule, argued rather than assumed. The approved CTA is
// self-referential here, so making it the closing button would give a
// convinced reader nowhere to go; "Meet the team" hands them to the one thing
// this page has spent 1,400 words saying no competitor can show them.

// CONTENT lives in src/content/pages/why-tlb.yaml and is edited in Pages CMS
// ("Why TLB"). This file owns the layout. The notes above explain why each
// section says what it says; they stay here because Pages CMS rewrites the
// YAML on save and would drop them.
const page = await getPage(
  'why-tlb',
  z.object({
    seo: section.seo,
    hero: section.hero,
    // Its background photo sits behind the trust bar and this block together
    // (the Backdrop below), so it cannot be left empty.
    definition: section.textBlock.extend({ backgroundImage: section.backgroundPhoto }),
    pathways: section.pathwayCards,
    tradeOff: section.textGrid,
    resolution: section.textBlock,
    // The rows and columns are the shared table in Site settings, so only the
    // framing around them is this page's own.
    comparison: section.comparisonTable.omit({ columns: true, rows: true }),
    model: section.textBlock,
    whyPeopleStay: section.textBlock,
    switching: section.textBlock,
    objections: section.textGrid,
    timing: section.callout,
    contractors: section.linkList,
    reviews: section.sectionHeading,
    faq: section.faq,
    switchingFaq: section.faq,
    closingCta: section.callToAction,
  }),
);

const pageMeta = {
  ...page.seo,
  canonical: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/why-tlb/',
};

const business = localBusiness({
  description: pageMeta.description,
  areaServed: 'Northern Rivers NSW and Southern Gold Coast QLD',
  sameAs: googleBusinessProfileUrl ? [googleBusinessProfileUrl] : [],
});

const heroImage = page.hero.image && sized(page.hero.image, '4/3');

const pathwayCards = page.pathways.cards.map((card, i) => ({
  number: i + 1,
  ...card,
  image: sized(card.image, '4/3'),
}));

const tradeOffBlocks = page.tradeOff.items.map((item) => ({
  type: 'text' as const,
  ...item,
  tone: 'muted' as const,
}));

const objectionBlocks = page.objections.items.map((item) => ({ type: 'text' as const, ...item }));
---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="split-single-image"
      imagePosition="right"
      kicker={page.hero.kicker}
      headingLines={page.hero.headingLines}
      subheading={page.hero.subheading}
      lead={page.hero.lead}
      cta={page.hero.cta ?? quoteCta}
      image={heroImage}
    />

    {/* Trust bar + definition block share ONE photo behind a Cream scrim, so it is
        laid out across both — see the homepage for why a per-section
        background cannot do this. 90%, raised from 75% on 29 Sep 2026.
        Measured over this frame's own darkest region under the copy column,
        75% gave 4.31:1 — an AA FAIL. 90% gives 6.01:1. Backdrop.astro's
        `opacity` doc carries the method. Re-check it if the photo is swapped
        in Pages CMS. */}
    <Backdrop image={page.definition.backgroundImage} tint="muted" opacity={90} focus="top">
      <TrustBar variant="card" cards={trustBarCards} />
      <TextBlock
        theme="muted"
        align="left"
        heading={page.definition.heading}
        body={page.definition.body}
        cta={page.definition.cta}
      />
    </Backdrop>

    <PathwayCards heading={page.pathways.heading} headingAccent={page.pathways.headingAccent} cards={pathwayCards} />

    <ContentGrid heading={page.tradeOff.heading} lead={page.tradeOff.lead} columns={2} blocks={tradeOffBlocks} />

    {/* Photo behind the Dark Teal ground rather than a flat band: this is the
        line the page turns on, and it is the only dark section between two
        light ones. 82% scrim — the same figure the homepage's dark pivot block
        uses — keeps Off White copy at full contrast over the photo's bright
        window wall. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="dark"
      align="left"
      heading={page.resolution.heading}
      body={page.resolution.body}
      cta={page.resolution.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.resolution.backgroundImage}
      scrimOpacity={82}
    />

    {/* Anchor target for §3's card 1. ComparisonTable takes no id prop, and
        adding one purely for an anchor isn't worth a shared-component change
        — same call made on deep-cleaning.astro. */}
    <div id="compare">
      <ComparisonTable
        heading={page.comparison.heading}
        lead={page.comparison.lead}
        cornerLabel={page.comparison.cornerLabel}
        columns={comparisonColumns}
        rows={comparisonRows}
        footnote={page.comparison.footnote}
        cta={page.comparison.cta}
      />
    </div>

    <TextBlock
      theme="light"
      align="left"
      heading={page.model.heading}
      body={page.model.body}
      cta={page.model.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.model.backgroundImage}
    />

    <TextBlock
      theme="dark"
      align="left"
      heading={page.whyPeopleStay.heading}
      body={page.whyPeopleStay.body}
      cta={page.whyPeopleStay.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.whyPeopleStay.backgroundImage}
    />

    {/* Anchor target for §3's card 2. */}
    <div id="switching">
      <TextBlock
        theme="light"
        align="left"
        heading={page.switching.heading}
        body={page.switching.body}
        cta={page.switching.cta}
        backgroundImage={page.switching.backgroundImage}
      />
    </div>

    <ContentGrid heading={page.objections.heading} lead={page.objections.lead} columns={2} blocks={objectionBlocks} />

    <Callout tone="warning" heading={page.timing.heading} body={page.timing.body} cta={page.timing.cta} />

    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="list"
      heading={page.contractors.heading}
      lead={page.contractors.lead}
      note={page.contractors.note}
      items={page.contractors.items}
    />

    <GoogleReviewSlider heading={page.reviews.heading} reviews={googleReviews} profileUrl={googleBusinessProfileUrl} />

    {/* ⚙️ SEO REWRITE (17 Sep 2026) — two FAQ headings, both replaced.
        "Frequently Asked Questions" was generic and made the split between the
        two blocks look like a build error; this block is about working with
        TLB, the next is about switching in general. "Switching Cleaners:
        FAQs" was Title Case, which breaks §9.2's sentence-case rule, and the
        colon read as a label rather than a heading. The questions inside both
        blocks are untouched. */}
    <Faq heading={page.faq.heading} lead={page.faq.lead} items={page.faq.items} />
    <Faq heading={page.switchingFaq.heading} lead={page.switchingFaq.lead} items={page.switchingFaq.items} />

    <CallToAction
      variant="secondary"
      kicker={page.closingCta.kicker}
      heading={page.closingCta.heading}
      lead={page.closingCta.lead}
      cta={page.closingCta.cta ?? quoteCta}
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
